# Build-context security boundary. `next build` resolves its trace root to the
# WHOLE project root — lib/storage.ts builds a cwd-relative path from
# LOCAL_STORAGE_DIR — so .next/standalone (and the web image built from it) picks
# up everything sitting next to the source, and the worker image gets the same
# tree verbatim via `COPY . .`. Anything present here at build time ships in the
# image unless it is excluded in THIS file: keep it tight.
node_modules
.next
.git
.DS_Store
data
backups
*.db
*.db-journal
prisma/*.db
prisma/*.db-journal
# The same names at ANY depth (pass 61). A pattern without `/` is anchored to
# the context root, so a SQLite file the tracer or a developer put under lib/ or
# scripts/ stayed in the context and shipped in the worker image. The Dockerfile
# prunes recursively too; this keeps the bytes out of the context entirely.
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
# Every dotenv flavour, at ANY DEPTH. A pattern without `/` is anchored to the
# context root and `*` does not cross directories, so `.env*` alone left
# `prisma/.env` (a supported Prisma convention, and the place an operator may
# keep DATABASE_URL) in the build context — `next build` traced it into the
# standalone tree and the web stage's `cp prisma/` re-added it AFTER the prune,
# while the worker's `COPY` carried it too. `**/` is what makes this rule
# recursive; the explicit unignore keeps the sanitized example. Both forms are
# listed deliberately: `.env*` pins the root, `**/.env*` reaches every nested
# copy, and neither depends on how `**` treats the zero-directory case.
.env*
**/.env*
!.env.example
test-results
playwright-report
coverage
research/darktest
designs/pixelduo/preview
# Generated user content (rendered images, cached outputs): runtime output, not
# source. `.gitignore` lists it for the same reason — one deployment's users'
# images must not be baked into an image that ships anywhere else.
public/generated
# Build/test output, regenerated on every build.
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo
out
dist
# Measurement tooling from the performance sprint: bench scripts, raw
# measurements and reports. Nothing at runtime reads any of it, and it is not
# inert — perf/bench/session.json is the bench's Playwright storage state, i.e. a
# LIVE `next-auth.session-token` cookie for whatever host mksession.py was
# pointed at (it takes a --base URL, and the committed default is the local dev
# server). `.gitignore` keeps that file out of git for exactly this reason; the
# build context is the other way an image carries a file off the host, and
# `COPY . .` (worker) plus the standalone trace (web) both reach it. The
# Dockerfile prunes perf/ as well — this file stops it entering the context at
# all.
perf
# Editor/agent state a BUILD HOST may carry (pass 31). `.gitignore` lists these
# precisely because they exist on a working machine, and that is the trap: they
# are invisible to every check that asks git what is in the tree, while
# .vscode/settings.json, .vscode/launch.json and .idea/workspace.xml routinely
# hold pasted tokens, private hostnames and local paths. The worker stage takes
# whatever is in the context (the bind-mount cp), so both filters must name
# them — this file keeps them out of the context, the Dockerfile's prune list
# names them for any path that bypasses this file.
.vscode
.idea
.claude
.cursor
*.swp
